Gibbs free energy deppends on temperature. We have addressed differences between standard and equilibrium pressure conditions. Let us now address the impact of temperature, as $\Delta G_R(P, T)$. On one hand, for reactions in which $K_c$ is larger than one (exergonic reactions in standard conditions), increasing temperature will favour spontaneity, as $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ overall increases with temperature. 
On the other hand, for reactions in which $K_c$ is smaller than one (endergonic reactions in standard conditions), increasing temperature will disfavour spontaneity, hence decreasing $\Delta G_R(P, T)$.






